            <ArticleTitle>Adverse reactions to intrathecal saline injection for control of pain.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>557-61</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The authors report the results of a questionnaire regarding the use of hypertonic saline for the control of pain. Of 2105 patients so treated, nearly 11% had an adverse temporary symptom or sign, reported as an untoward reaction; slightly over 1% suffered a significant morbidity, of which paraplegia or quadriplegia was by far the most common, and two patients died (0.1%).</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
